Interaction models aim to enhance human-AI collaboration by enabling multimodal, real-time interactions across audio, video, and text.

Very excited to share a preview of what we’ve been working on! 

@thinkymachines: "People talk, listen, watch, think, and collaborate at the same time, in real time. We've designed an AI that works with people the same way. We share our approach, early results, and a quick look at our model in action."

Interaction models move beyond turn-based AI interfaces by handling multimodal, real-time collaboration natively across audio, video, and text.

In the past few months, we had a lot of fun (and stress) producing 12 versions and 137 pages in our training run log book. It turns out that human-human collaboration is important for improving human-AI collaboration.

@thinkymachines: "People talk, listen, watch, think, and collaborate at the same time, in real time. We've designed an AI that works with people the same way. We share our approach, early results, and a quick look at our model in action." 

Interaction models move beyond turn-based AI interfaces by handling multimodal, real-time collaboration natively across audio, video, and text.

Congrats to Rowan and the Thinky team on the cool release! It's exciting to see this release go beyond just a single model, showcasing truly different native multimodal interactions.

A couple of points from the nicely written blog resonate: people are most effective when they can collaborate with AI the same way they do with other people, and existing interfaces limit human inputs, especially multimodal ones, which need to be lifted to unlock much better interactivity. The blog also reminds me of the fun and challenging discussions with @shannonzshen and others on what 'scaling collaboration' can look like. It would be great to see more human-centric evaluations of the model/system/interface too — looking forward to it.

